content.text:
I've fixed it: in scoped native CSS, declarations sitting directly inside an `@media` (or `@container` or other at-rule) under a nested parent rule now get the scope id. Applied to the issue's example, it now produces the expected output:

```css
.foo {
  &[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
  .bar[data-v-test] {}
  @media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
    &[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
    .bar[data-v-test] { color: red }
  }
}
```

**Cause:** When a scoped rule contains nested rules, Vue moves that rule's own declarations into a `& { … }` block so the scope id can be attached there. It never did this for at-rules directly inside that rule. So `background-color: green` inside `@media` stayed bare, had no scope id, and lost to the scoped `red`.

**Fix** (in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`):
- I added a helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`, that moves a node's direct declarations into a `& { … }` block.
- It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule directly inside it, so declarations inside `@media` and `@container` are scoped the same way.

One behaviour change: the helper also moves comments into the `&` block along with declarations, where the old code moved only declarations. A rule or at-rule whose only direct content is comments will now get a `& { /* … */ }` block. That's harmless in the browser, but output snapshots could differ.

**Checks:** the existing `compileStyle` tests pass (25/25). The output above came from a temporary test that I deleted afterwards, so I haven't added a test for this case. TypeScript shows no errors in the changed file.

